            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 1)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "5627dc6e-c943-439e-ab4a-4531b7f24264"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of J. Robert Oppenheimer</strong><br>\nJ. Robert Oppenheimer was born in New York City in 1904 to a wealthy family. He showed early signs of a brilliant mind, excelling in languages and science. At Harvard, he majored in chemistry but later shifted his focus to physics, pursuing graduate studies at Cambridge University. However, he found his true calling at the University of Göttingen, which was a leading center for theoretical physics during his time.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Rise as a Physicist</strong><br>\nOppenheimer's rise in the field of physics was rapid. By the mid-1930s, he had already made significant contributions to the theories of electrons and positrons, and quantum mechanics. His work earned him a reputation as a promising young scientist, and he began teaching at the University of California, Berkeley. There, he influenced a generation of physicists with his broad knowledge and engaging lectures.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Manhattan Project</strong><br>\nThe onset of World War II turned Oppenheimer's attention to a new endeavor: the development of nuclear weapons. He was appointed as the scientific director of the Manhattan Project, the United States' secret effort to build the atomic bomb. He led the project's laboratory at Los Alamos, New Mexico, overseeing the work that led to the creation of the first nuclear bombs.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
