            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Trinity Test</strong><br>\nUnder Oppenheimer's leadership, the Manhattan Project culminated in the Trinity test, the first detonation of a nuclear weapon, in the New Mexico desert in July 1945. This test was a pivotal moment in history, demonstrating the devastating power of atomic energy. Oppenheimer famously recalled a line from the Bhagavad Gita: \"Now I am become Death, the destroyer of worlds,\" reflecting on the magnitude of what he had helped create.</p>\n\n<p><strong>After the War</strong><br>\nAfter the war, Oppenheimer became a chief advisor to the newly created United States Atomic Energy Commission. He advocated for international control of nuclear power and opposed the development of the hydrogen bomb, a stance that later led to controversy. His opposition to the H-bomb and his past associations with leftist movements raised suspicions during the Red Scare.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Red Scare</strong><br>\nDuring the late 1940s and early 1950s, America was swept by a wave of anti-communist sentiment known as the Red Scare. Oppenheimer was one of many intellectuals whose past affiliations were scrutinized. In 1954, his security clearance was revoked in a highly publicized hearing that examined his previous political associations and his stance on military policy.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
