          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Later Years and Reflection</strong><br>\nAfter losing his security clearance, Oppenheimer spent the remaining years of his career focusing on education and philosophy of science. He became the director of the Institute for Advanced Study in Princeton, New Jersey, where he worked until his death. Despite his fall from grace in the eyes of some government officials, he continued to be a prominent voice in science and philosophy.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Recognition and Legacy</strong><br>\nOppenheimer's contributions to physics and his role in the Manhattan Project left a lasting impact on science and history. He received numerous awards and honors, including the Enrico Fermi Award, granted by President Lyndon B. Johnson. Despite the controversies he faced, his legacy as a \"father of the atomic bomb\" remains complex and significant.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Posthumous Reflection</strong><br>\nOppenheimer passed away from throat cancer in 1967, but his legacy continues to provoke debate and reflection. The moral and ethical questions surrounding the creation and use of nuclear weapons are part of his controversial legacy. His life story has been the subject of many books and films, symbolizing the intersection of scientific genius and the responsibility that comes with it.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
